   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Julia Wang \u2014 diversification versus economic complexity\n",
    "\n",
    "**Research question: Do more diversified countries always have higher ECI?**\n",
    "\n",
    "We compare export-share entropy (how evenly exports are distributed), RCA diversity (number of groups with RCA > 1), and ECI (the structure of the country\u2013industry specialization network). HHI is a secondary concentration measure; higher HHI means less diversification.\n",
    "\n",
    "Data: existing `data/cleaned/2019clean.csv` through `2023clean.csv`. No downloads or GDP required. Primary year: 2019. Repeat across years and compare fixed versus available country samples.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Method\n",
    "\n",
    "Exclude the overlapping High-complexity Products aggregate, retain zero entries, and remove countries with zero total exports. The primary sample is the intersection of country names with positive exports in every year. Missing and negative data trigger errors. We use 17 remaining groups; these retain the lab's broad classification limitations.\n",
    "\n",
    "RCA is an industry's country export share divided by its sample-wide export share. Presence is RCA > 1. ECI is the standardized second eigenvector of the usual country operator, calculated through its symmetric equivalent. Its sign is oriented positively to RCA diversity by convention. Thus the sign of that particular association is not independent evidence. This is recomputed grouped-data ECI, not published Atlas ECI.\n"
   ],
   "id": "julia-0"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "import sys\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "from IPython.display import display, SVG, Markdown\n",
    "REPO = next(p for p in (Path.cwd(), *Path.cwd().parents) if (p / \"juliawang\" / \"analyze.py\").exists())\n",
    "sys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / \"juliawang\"))\n",
    "from analyze import run\n",
    "results, correlations = run()\n"
   ],
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "id": "julia-1"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Compare diversification with ECI\n",
    "\n",
    "Spearman correlation compares country rankings. A correlation below 1 means the two measures do not produce identical orderings."
   ],
   "id": "julia-2"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "display(correlations.loc[correlations[\"sample\"] == \"common\"])\n",
    "display(SVG(filename=str(REPO / \"juliawang/outputs/2019_diversification_vs_eci.svg\")))\n"
   ],
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "id": "julia-3"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Examine disagreements\n",
    "\n",
    "The next table identifies the largest absolute differences in entropy and ECI ranks. A positive rank gap means ECI ranks the country higher than entropy does. These examples explain disagreement; they are not evidence that one measure is correct.\n"
   ],
   "id": "julia-4"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "table = pd.read_csv(REPO / \"juliawang/outputs/2019_largest_rank_disagreements.csv\")\n",
    "display(table)\n",
    "violations = pd.read_csv(REPO / \"juliawang/outputs/2019_entropy_ordering_violations.csv\")\n",
    "display(violations[[\"Country_a\", \"Country_b\", \"entropy_a\", \"entropy_b\", \"eci_a\", \"eci_b\"]].head(10))\n"
   ],
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "id": "julia-5"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Same RCA count, different ECI\n",
    "\n",
    "Countries may specialize in the same number of groups while specializing in different groups. Compare their export profiles before interpreting the difference.\n"
   ],
   "id": "julia-6"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "r = results[2019]\n",
    "spread = r.groupby(\"rca_diversity\").agg(countries=(\"eci\", \"size\"), min_eci=(\"eci\", \"min\"), max_eci=(\"eci\", \"max\"))\n",
    "spread[\"eci_range\"] = spread.max_eci - spread.min_eci\n",
    "display(spread.loc[spread.countries >= 2].sort_values(\"eci_range\", ascending=False))\n"
   ],
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "id": "julia-7"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Results and limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "Pair comparisons share countries and are not independent observations. This is descriptive analysis, not a causal or growth-prediction test. The repository flags possible 2023 data anomalies, so use 2019 as primary. Changing prices can change export shares. The fixed sample uses exact recorded country names without harmonization. ECI is standardized separately each year.\n",
    "\n",
    "Next experiments: inspect countries with similar entropy but different ECI; inspect which industries distinguish them; vary the RCA cutoff; compare the available-sample results with the primary fixed sample. Avoid claiming to measure productive capabilities directly.\n"
    "\n"
    "More diversified countries tend to have higher ECI, but diversification alone does not determine complexity. In our 2019 grouped dataset, simple diversification models explain approximately 11–16% of ECI variation.\n"
   ],
   "id": "julia-8"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "display(Markdown((REPO / \"juliawang/outputs/findings.md\").read_text()))"
   ],
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "id": "julia-9"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "id": "models-intro",
   "source": [
    "## 5. Plot descriptive models\n",
    "Compare linear and quadratic fits for entropy, RCA diversity, and HHI. R\u00b2 is in-sample fit; leave-one-country-out RMSE is a reconstruction check, not growth forecasting or causal evidence. ECI was computed from the joint country network.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
